  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with a custom GLSL water surface and a gradient sky dome. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it or `vite build` here, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. The shader compiles only in the browser, and a compile error would skip the draw silently, so check that the water and sky actually appear.\n\n- **Water geometry:** the surface is a 250×250 subdivided grid, 200 units wide. A vertex shader displaces it with four directional sine waves and computes the normals analytically. A `waveClock` uniform, written every frame, drives the animation.\n- **Water shading:**\n  - Fresnel mixes the water body colour with the reflected sky, so grazing angles show more sky.\n  - Noise ripples come from value-noise fbm. They perturb the normal, scroll with time and fade with distance.\n  - A sun glint uses a sharp and a broad Blinn-Phong lobe.\n  - Haze blends the far water into the horizon colour.\n- **Sky:** a large back-face sphere with its own gradient shader, a sun disc and a halo. The water samples the same sky function for its reflection.\n- **Camera:** it sits about 3 units above the water at z=22 and looks toward the horizon.\n- **Colour:** all shader colours are linear light, and the app sets `output: { toneMapping: \"none\" }`. The sky and water colours should therefore show as authored instead of being darkened by ACES tone mapping.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\n",
